      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (before the column-selection cell):**\n",
        "The merged table has 100 columns, but most of them are race-by-gender breakdowns of the outcomes (for example, outcomes for Black women or Hispanic men) that I will not use in this project. I keep the **pooled** outcomes: mean household income rank (`kfr_pooled_pooled_p25`, my main outcome), the incarceration rate (`jail_pooled_pooled_p25`, a possible second outcome), the standard error of the income rank, and the number of children (`pooled_pooled_count`, which tells me how reliable each tract's estimate is). For predictors I keep a set of neighborhood characteristics tied to my question: income, poverty, education, family structure, race and ethnicity, commuting, density, jobs, and school performance. Where a measure exists for several years I keep one year (mostly 2010) so the columns are not redundant."
      ]
