      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (decisions on outliers):**\n",
        "- **Population density and job density:** both are very skewed (job density has 97 IQR outliers and a maximum around 244,096; population density has 52 outliers). These extreme values are real: they are dense downtown tracts and job centers, not errors, so I **keep** them. Because the skew is strong, I plan to apply a log transform in the EDA step.\n",
        "- **Median household income:** the maximum, 250,001, looks like a Census top-coded ceiling (the highest value the survey reports), shared by 3 tract(s). It is not a true income figure, so I keep the value but will remember that very high-income tracts are capped.\n",
        "- **Small tracts:** tracts with fewer than 20 children have much noisier estimates (the table shows the standard error is largest in the smallest group), so I dropped 9 tracts below that cutoff. I can change `MIN_KIDS` if my mentor prefers a different threshold, or later use `pooled_pooled_count` as a weight.\n",
        "- **Negative incarceration rates:** 97 tracts still have a negative incarceration rate. The codebook explains that small random noise was added to every estimate to protect privacy, which can push values near zero below zero. I created `jail_clipped` (negatives set to 0) and will treat incarceration only as a secondary outcome. The main outcome is the income rank."
      ]
    },
